be two more chows. But I have B4, B6, and B7. If I can get B5 and B8, but B8 is available (4 left). However, I have only one B7. Alternatively, maybe I can form a pung of B4 or B6. Since I have two each, if I draw another, that would make a pung. But that would require another B4 or B6.\n\nLooking at the available actions, if I play B4, I lose the chance to form a pung. Similarly for B6. Playing B7 might be safe since I have only one, but maybe someone else needs it. Let's see the game history. The previous player has played W5, B6, etc. The player across has a meld of T6, T7, T8 and W7, W8, W9. The next player has played J1, F3, etc.\n\nIf I discard B7, maybe someone can use it for a chow. But since I have only one, it's not part of a pung. Alternatively, if I keep B7 and draw another B7, that would form a pair. But the available B7 count is 3. If I discard it now, I can't form a pair later.\n\nAnother angle: My current hand has two pairs? Let's see. B4x2 and B6x2. So that's two pairs. If I can form two more pairs, maybe I can get to seven pairs. But I have W7, T4, T9, and B7. If I can pair those, but that's unlikely. Alternatively, maybe I can form a seven pairs hand. But with the current tiles, I have two pairs (B4, B6) and four singles. Not enough.\n\nLooking at the scoring patterns, maybe I can aim for \"All Simples\" (no terminals or honors). My hand has W7 (terminal?), T4, T9 (terminals), B4, B6, B7. W7 is a 7, which is a simple. T4 is a 4, simple. T9 is a terminal. So T9 is a terminal. So \"All Simples\" would require no terminals. Since I have T9, that pattern is out. Hmm.\n\nAlternatively, maybe \"All Chows\". If I can form four chows and a pair. Currently, I have two chows. If I can form two more chows in B suit. For example, B4, B5, B6 and B6, B7, B8. Then a pair of B4 or B6. But I need to have those tiles. If I discard B4 or B6, I might break that possibility.\n\nAnother possibility: \"Pure Shifted Chows\" if I can form three consecutive chows in the same suit with a fixed interval. But I'm not sure. Let's see. If I have B4, B5, B6 and B6, B7, B8, that's two chows. If I can get B2, B3, B4, but I don't have those. Not sure.\n\nAlternatively, maybe \"Outside Hand\", which requires terminals and honors in every meld and pair. But my current hand has T9 (terminal) and maybe some chows. Not sure.\n\nLooking at the valid actions, the best move might be to discard a tile that's less critical. Let's see: B4 and B6 are each two copies. If I discard B4, maybe someone can take it for a chow. But since I have two, maybe it's safe to discard one. Similarly for B6. Alternatively, T4 is a single, so discarding it might be safe. T9 is a terminal, which might be needed for someone else's hand.\n\nThe available actions are Play B7, T9, W7, B4, B6, T4. Let's evaluate each:\n\n- Play B7: Discarding B7. Since I have only one, it's a single. If someone needs it for a chow, they can take it. But I don't have others to form a pung. Maybe safe.\n\n- Play T9: T9 is a terminal. If someone is building a terminal-based hand, they might need it. But since it's a terminal, it's part of many patterns. Discarding it might be risky.\n\n- Play W7: I have one W7 in hand. The melds already include W7 in the [W6, W7, W8] chow. If I discard W7, someone might take it for a pung or another chow. But since the meld is already made, maybe it's safe. However, if I keep it, maybe I can form a pair later.\n\n- Play B4: Discard one of the two B4s. If I can form a pung later, but with two left, maybe safe.\n\n- Play B6: Similar to B4. Discard one of the two B6s.\n\n- Play T4: Single tile, discard it. T4 could be part of a chow, but I don't have T3 or T5. Maybe safe.\n\nConsidering the game history, the previous player has played W5, B6, etc. The player across has a meld of T6, T7, T8 and W7, W8, W9. The next player has played T6. If I discard T9, the player across might need it to complete their W7, W8, W9 chow? No, they have W7, W8, W9 as a chow already. Wait,