n get B5, I can form a chow of B4, B5, B6. Since B5 has 2 left, it's possible. If I keep B4 and B6, I can wait for B5. If I discard B4, I lose that chance. Similarly, if I discard B6, I lose the chance for B5 and B6. So maybe it's better to keep both B4 and B6 and wait for B5.\n\nSo, in that case, the best move is to discard a tile that's not part of a potential chow. T4 is a single, and not part of any potential chow I can form. So discarding T4 is the safest. That way, I keep B4 and B6 for possible chows with B5. Also, T4 is a middle tile, which might be less likely to be needed for a chow compared to B4 or B6.\n\nTherefore, the best action is to Play T4.\n</think>\n\nEvaluation: Discarding T4 is the safest move as it's a middle tile not part of any potential chow and preserves B4/B6 for possible B4-B5-B6 chow.  \nReason: Keeping B4 and B6 allows forming a chow with B5 if drawn, while T4 is less critical and reduces risk of aiding opponents' melds.  \nAnswer: Play T4"}
