s less likely to be needed. Since the player across has T6-T7-T8, they might be looking for T9. If I discard T9, they can Chi it. But if I discard T1, maybe they don't need it. \n\nAlternatively, the W7 is part of a meld, but since there are two left, maybe it's safe. \n\nWait, the W7 is part of the meld [W6, W7, W8]. So if I discard W7, the previous player has a meld of W6-W7-W8, so they already have that. Maybe they don't need it. \n\nSo, between the options, maybe playing J2 is safer. Let me check the game history. The previous player has played J2 twice, so they might have two. If I discard J2, they could Peng it. But if they already have two, they might be looking for the third. \n\nAlternatively, if I discard B4, since there are four left, it's less likely someone needs it. \n\nBut considering all factors, the safest tile to discard might be J2, as it's a single tile and there are two left. Playing J2 would be the answer.\n</think>\n\nEvaluation: The safest tile to discard is J2, as it's a single tile with two remaining in the wall, reducing the chance of opponents completing melds. Other tiles like T9 or B6 might be needed by players with visible melds, increasing the risk of being called.\nReason: Discarding J2 minimizes the risk of opponents completing their hands, especially since the previous player has already played two J2s. This move avoids giving a third J2 to any player who might need it for a pung.\nAnswer: Play J2"}
