e user has one J3 in their hand. \n\nThe possible patterns that could apply are:\n\n- All Chows: If the hand is all chows and a pair. But the user has a pung of B4, which is a pung, not a chow. So All Chows would not apply. \n\n- All Pungs: The user has a pung of B4. If the other tiles form pungs, but the hand has W3, W7, W9, B6, J3. These are not pungs. So All Pungs would not apply. \n\n- Mixed Straight: Three chows in different suits. The user has three chows in W and a pung of B4. Not applicable. \n\n- Pure Straight: Three chows in the same suit. The user has two chows in W (4-5-6 and 6-7-8). If there's a third chow, but the hand tiles include W3 and W9. If the user plays W3, they might have a chow of W3-4-5. But the user already has a W4 in the meld. So if they play W3, they could form a chow of W3-4-5. But the user already has a chow of 4-5-6. So playing W3 would add a chow of 3-4-5, making three chows in W. That would be a Pure Straight (3 chows in same suit), which is a 16-point pattern. \n\nWait, the user's current melds include two chows in W: 4-5-6 and 6-7-8. If they play W3, they would have a chow of 3-4-5 (if they have W3, W4, W5). But the user has a W4 in the meld [W4, W5, W6]. So if they play W3, they would have W3, and since they already have W4 and W5 in the meld, that forms a chow of 3-4-5. So with three chows in W (3-4-5, 4-5-6, 6-7-8), that's three chows in the same suit, which is a Pure Straight (16 points). Additionally, the pung of B4 would count as another pung. But the hand would then have three chows and a pung. Wait, the total melds would be four (three chows and one pung) plus a pair. But the user's hand would need to have four melds and a pair. If the user plays W3, they would have three chows (3-4-5, 4-5-6, 6-7-8) and the pung of B4, totaling four melds, and the remaining tile would be a pair. Let's see: the hand tiles after playing W3 would be W7, W9, B6, J3. But that's four tiles. Wait, no, the user would have played one tile, so the h